"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"Covers both directions correctly. Mutual fund cash redemptions force shared sales, gains and costs borne by ETF holders. Cash-settled ETF flows or ETF-driven trading push costs onto mutual fund holders. The answer also correctly treats in-kind removal of low-basis lots as a shared benefit, not a harmful subsidy. It names the three monitoring categories (transaction costs, cash, gains) and avoids inventing universal numeric thresholds, deferring to board-approved policy. However, it misreads the 'representative order-specific framework' as trade-order tagging and causal fill allocation rather than the exemptive order/application conditions. That framework is board-approved thresholds, methods and periods for transaction costs, cash levels and capital-gains distributions. The answer's repeated claim that costs are 'allocated by originating class' mischaracterizes the shared-portfolio framework and is not part of the representative conditions. It also omits that a threshold breach prompts notice and assessment rather than automatic illegality or closure. It does not mention that the cost burden is measured net of transaction charges. Useful central conceptual content, but the framework section has a substantive error and omissions.\"}","ttft_ms":3031,"type":"result","duration_ms":6589,"uuid":"fb2e7aca-a7b9-4fbb-96a3-07bb48a8d945","ttft_stream_ms":513,"time_to_request_ms":63,"first_content_frame_ms":513,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
